a = {"title": "Impact of LDLR and PCSK9 pathogenic variants in Japanese heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia patients", "authors": ["Mika Hori", "Naotaka Ohta", "Atsushi Takahashi", "Hiroaki Masuda", "Rieko Isoda", "Suguru Yamamoto", "Cheol Son", "Masatsune Ogura", "Kiminori Hosoda", "Yoshihiro Miyamoto", "Mariko Harada-Shiba"], "section_header": "ABSTRACT", "subsection_header": "null", "sub_subsection_header": "null", "genes_mentioned": ["LDLR", "PCSK9"], "variant_count": 5, "variants": [{"gene": "LDLR", "genomic_variant": "null", "transcript_variant": "c.1845+2T>C", "exon": "null", "protein_variant": "null", "protein_domain": "null", "clinical_information": "Patients harbouring this variant had significantly lower serum LDL-cholesterol levels compared with those harbouring other LDLR pathogenic variants", "clinical_significance": "Pathogenic", "frequency": "7.5% (n=27)", "het_carriers": "27", "hom_carriers": "null", "affected_carriers": "27", "unaffected_carriers": "null", "number_of_meioses": "null"}, {"gene": "LDLR", "genomic_variant": "null", "transcript_variant": "c.1012T>A", "exon": "null", "protein_variant": "p.(Cys338Ser)", "protein_domain": "null", "clinical_information": "One of five frequent LDLR pathogenic variants found in 17% of unrelated FH patients", "clinical_significance": "Pathogenic", "frequency": "6.4% (n=23)", "het_carriers": "23", "hom_carriers": "null", "affected_carriers": "23", "unaffected_carriers": "null", "number_of_meioses": "null"}, {"gene": "LDLR", "genomic_variant": "null", "transcript_variant": "c.1297G>C", "exon": "null", "protein_variant": "p.(Asp433His)", "protein_domain": "null", "clinical_information": "One of five frequent LDLR pathogenic variants found in 17% of unrelated FH patients", "clinical_significance": "Pathogenic", "frequency": "5.5% (n=20)", "het_carriers": "20", "hom_carriers": "null", "affected_carriers": "20", "unaffected_carriers": "null", "number_of_meioses": "null"}, {"gene": "LDLR", "genomic_variant": "null", "transcript_variant": "c.1702C>G", "exon": "null", "protein_variant": "p.(Leu568Val)", "protein_domain": "null", "clinical_information": "Patients harbouring this variant had significantly higher serum HDL-cholesterol levels compared with those harbouring other LDLR pathogenic variants", "clinical_significance": "Pathogenic", "frequency": "5.2% (n=19)", "het_carriers": "19", "hom_carriers": "null", "affected_carriers": "19", "unaffected_carriers": "null", "number_of_meioses": "null"}, {"gene": "LDLR", "genomic_variant": "null", "transcript_variant": "c.2431A>T", "exon": "null", "protein_variant": "p.(Lys811*)", "protein_domain": "null", "clinical_information": "One of five frequent LDLR pathogenic variants found in 17% of unrelated FH patients", "clinical_significance": "Pathogenic", "frequency": "5.8% (n=21)", "het_carriers": "21", "hom_carriers": "null", "affected_carriers": "21", "unaffected_carriers": "null", "number_of_meioses": "null"}], "path": "/Users/arjun/PycharmProjects/VI_RAG/VI_RAG/tools/Hori_et_al_2019_PMID_31491741.md", "chunk_idx": 1, "chunk": "Background and aims: More than 4970 variants in the low-density lipoprotein receptor (LDLR) gene and 350 variants in the proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin 9 (PCSK9) gene have been reported in familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) patients. However, the effects of these variants on FH pathophysiology have not been fully clarified. We aimed to update the LDLR and PCSK9 variants in Japanese heterozygous FH (HeFH) patients and annotate their clinical significance for the genetic diagnosis of HeFH. Methods: A genetic analysis of the LDLR and PCSK9 genes was performed in 801 clinically diagnosed HeFH patients. The association of the pathogenic variants with the clinical FH phenotype was examined. Results: Pathogenic variants in the LDLR and PCSK9 genes were found in 46% (n = 296) and 7.8% (n = 51) of unrelated FH patients (n = 650), respectively. The prevalence of Achilles tendon thickness was low (44%) in patients harbouring PCSK9 pathogenic variants. Furthermore, 17% of unrelated FH patients harboured one of five frequent LDLR pathogenic variants: c.1845+2T>C, c.1012T>A: p.(Cys338Ser), c.1297G>C: p.(Asp433His), c.1702C>G: p.(Leu568Val), and c.2431A>T: p.(Lys811*). Patients harbouring the c.1845+2T>C and c.1702C>G: p.(Leu568Val) variants had significantly lower serum LDL-cholesterol levels and higher serum HDL-cholesterol levels, respectively, compared with those harbouring the other LDLR pathogenic variants. The proportion of LDLR pathogenic variants was higher in patients with a younger age of coronary artery disease (CAD) onset and significantly decreased as the age of CAD onset increased. Conclusions: This study annotated the clinical significance and characteristics of LDLR and PCSK9 pathogenic variants in Japanese HeFH patients.", "description": "This study aimed to update and annotate the clinical significance of LDLR and PCSK9 variants in Japanese heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia (HeFH) patients to improve genetic diagnosis and clarify pathophysiological effects. Genetic analysis was conducted on 801 clinically diagnosed HeFH patients, including 650 unrelated individuals. Pathogenic variants in the LDLR gene were identified in 46% (n=296) of unrelated patients, while PCSK9 pathogenic variants were found in 7.8% (n=51). Five frequent LDLR pathogenic variants were observed in 17% of unrelated patients: c.1845+2T>C, c.1012T>A:p.(Cys338Ser), c.1297G>C:p.(Asp433His), c.1702C>G:p.(Leu568Val), and c.2431A>T:p.(Lys811*). Clinical phenotype associations revealed that patients harboring the c.1845+2T>C variant had significantly lower serum LDL-cholesterol levels, whereas those with the c.1702C>G:p.(Leu568Val) variant exhibited higher serum HDL-cholesterol levels compared to carriers of other LDLR pathogenic variants. The prevalence of Achilles tendon thickness was low (44%) in patients with PCSK9 pathogenic variants. Furthermore, the proportion of LDLR pathogenic variants was inversely correlated with the age of coronary artery disease (CAD) onset, being significantly higher in patients with younger CAD onset and decreasing as the age of onset increased. The study concludes that annotating these variants provides critical data for the accurate genetic diagnosis and risk assessment of Japanese HeFH patients."}

b = {"title": "Impact of LDLR and PCSK9 pathogenic variants in Japanese heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia patients", "authors": ["Mika Hori", "Naotaka Ohta", "Atsushi Takahashi", "Hiroaki Masuda", "Rieko Isoda", "Suguru Yamamoto", "Cheol Son", "Masatsune Ogura", "Kiminori Hosoda", "Yoshihiro Miyamoto", "Mariko Harada-Shiba"], "section_header": "1. Introduction", "subsection_header": "null", "sub_subsection_header": "null", "genes_mentioned": ["LDLR", "APOB", "PCSK9"], "variant_count": 0, "variants": [], "path": "/Users/arjun/PycharmProjects/VI_RAG/VI_RAG/tools/Hori_et_al_2019_PMID_31491741.md", "chunk_idx": 1, "chunk": "Familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) is characterized by high low-density lipoprotein-cholesterol (LDL-C) levels, skin and tendon xanthomas and premature coronary artery disease (CAD), and its prevalence is 1 heterozygous FH (HeFH) patient per 200-500 individuals in the general population. FH patients have an extremely high risk of developing CAD; therefore, an accurate diagnosis of FH should be made as early as possible, a treatment strategy suitable to the patient should then be started as early as possible. FH is caused by mutations in the low-density lipoprotein receptor (LDLR), apolipoprotein B (APOB) and proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 9 (PCSK9) genes, but mutations in the APOB gene have not been reported in Japanese FH patients. Gain-of-function variants in the PCSK9 gene induce further degradation of LDLR and a subsequent increase in the serum LDL-C levels, whereas loss-of-function variants cause low LDL-C levels by increasing LDL clearance and reducing the risk of CAD. Over 4970 variants in the LDLR gene and 350 variants in the PCSK9 gene related to FH are described in ClinVar; however, the clinical significance of the variants in the LDLR and PCSK9 genes has not been fully annotated. The characteristics of the detected variants show differences based on the country or race. In addition, LDLR and PCSK9 variants are incidentally detected via genome sequencing. Thus, it is very important to assess the pathogenicity of the variants detected in clinical settings or as incidental findings. In the present study, we updated the LDLR and PCSK9 variants in Japanese HeFH patients and examined the association of the FH phenotype with these variants.", "description": "The input text defines Familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) as a condition characterized by high low-density lipoprotein-cholesterol (LDL-C), xanthomas, and premature coronary artery disease (CAD), with a prevalence of 1 heterozygous FH (HeFH) patient per 200-500 individuals. It identifies LDLR, APOB, and PCSK9 as causative genes but notes APOB mutations are unreported in Japanese patients, a fact confirmed by the context study which excluded APOB analysis for this reason. The text highlights the existence of over 4970 LDLR and 350 PCSK9 variants in ClinVar with unannotated clinical significance and racial differences, stating the study aim to update variants in Japanese HeFH patients. The context markdown fulfills this aim, reporting LDLR pathogenic variants in 46% (n=296) and PCSK9 pathogenic variants in 7.8% (n=51) of 650 unrelated FH patients. Specific frequent LDLR variants identified include c.1845+2T>C, c.1012T>A:p.(Cys338Ser), c.1297G>C:p.(Asp433His), c.1702C>G:p.(Leu568Val), and c.2431A>T:p.(Lys811*), comprising 17% of unrelated patients. The predominant PCSK9 pathogenic variant was c.94G>A:p.(Glu32Lys). Clinical analysis from the context supports the input's emphasis on early diagnosis and risk, showing LDLR pathogenic variant carriers had significantly higher LDL-C and Achilles tendon thickness (ATT) than PCSK9 carriers or non-carriers. PCSK9 pathogenic variant carriers had a lower ATT prevalence (44%). Additionally, the proportion of LDLR pathogenic variants significantly decreased with increased age of CAD onset, supporting the input's statement on premature CAD risk. This validates the input text's conclusion on the importance of assessing pathogenicity in clinical settings due to phenotype associations and racial variant differences."}

c = {"title": "Impact of LDLR and PCSK9 pathogenic variants in Japanese heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia patients", "authors": ["Mika Hori", "Naotaka Ohta", "Atsushi Takahashi", "Hiroaki Masuda", "Rieko Isoda", "Suguru Yamamoto", "Cheol Son", "Masatsune Ogura", "Kiminori Hosoda", "Yoshihiro Miyamoto", "Mariko Harada-Shiba"], "section_header": "2. Materials and methods", "subsection_header": "2.1. Patients", "sub_subsection_header": "null", "genes_mentioned": [], "variant_count": 0, "variants": [], "path": "/Users/arjun/PycharmProjects/VI_RAG/VI_RAG/tools/Hori_et_al_2019_PMID_31491741.md", "chunk_idx": 1, "chunk": "A genetic analysis of 801 clinically diagnosed HeFH patients, 650 of whom were unrelated, was performed in the National Cerebral and Cardiovascular Center (NCVC) between May 2005 and January 2018. Five hundred forty-one patients (67.5%) visited the NCVC. The rest of the patients were referred from other hospitals throughout Japan for genetic analysis, and their blood samples were sent to the NCVC. The diagnosis of HeFH was made according to the Japanese FH guidelines, which entail the patients having at least two of the following factors: LDL-C>180 mg/dL, tendon/skin xanthomas, and familial history of FH or premature CAD within the second degree of kinship. After excluding 33 patients due to insufficient clinical information and 44 patients who were younger than 20 years, 724 patients were analysed to assess the association of clinical characteristics with gene variants. Of the 724 HeFH patients, 607 were unrelated. The protocol used in this study was approved by the Ethics Review Committee of the NCVC (M17-56). Each patient provided written informed consent to participate in the study.", "description": "The input text outlines the patient cohort and ethical framework detailed in Section 2.1 of the study Impact of LDLR and PCSK9 pathogenic variants in Japanese heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia patients. Genetic analysis was performed on 801 clinically diagnosed heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia (HeFH) patients, including 650 unrelated individuals, at the National Cerebral and Cardiovascular Center (NCVC) from May 2005 to January 2018. While 541 patients (67.5%) visited the NCVC, others were referred from hospitals throughout Japan with blood samples submitted to the NCVC. Diagnosis followed Japanese FH guidelines, necessitating at least two criteria: untreated LDL-C >180 mg/dL, tendon or skin xanthomas, or familial history of FH or premature coronary artery disease (CAD) within second-degree kinship. To assess associations between clinical characteristics and gene variants, 33 patients with insufficient information and 44 patients under 20 years old were excluded, yielding an analysis group of 724 patients (607 unrelated). The study was approved by the NCVC Ethics Review Committee (M17-56) with written informed consent obtained from all participants. Contextualizing this within the full study, this cohort enabled the annotation of LDLR and PCSK9 variants, identifying pathogenic variants in 46% and 7.8% of unrelated patients respectively, with frequent LDLR variants including c.1845+2T>C, c.1012T>A:p.(Cys338Ser), c.1297G>C:p.(Asp433His), c.1702C>G:p.(Leu568Val), and c.2431A>T:p.(Lys811*), and the predominant PCSK9 variant being c.94G>A:p.(Glu32Lys). APOB variants were not examined as they are unreported in Japanese FH patients. This clinical data facilitated findings such as the decreased proportion of LDLR pathogenic variants with increased age of CAD onset and lower Achilles tendon thickness prevalence in PCSK9 variant carriers compared to LDLR carriers."}

d = {"title": "Impact of LDLR and PCSK9 pathogenic variants in Japanese heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia patients", "authors": ["Mika Hori", "Naotaka Ohta", "Atsushi Takahashi", "Hiroaki Masuda", "Rieko Isoda", "Suguru Yamamoto", "Cheol Son", "Masatsune Ogura", "Kiminori Hosoda", "Yoshihiro Miyamoto", "Mariko Harada-Shiba"], "section_header": "2. Materials and methods", "subsection_header": "2.2. DNA analysis", "sub_subsection_header": "null", "genes_mentioned": ["LDLR", "PCSK9", "APOB"], "variant_count": 0, "variants": [], "path": "/Users/arjun/PycharmProjects/VI_RAG/VI_RAG/tools/Hori_et_al_2019_PMID_31491741.md", "chunk_idx": 1, "chunk": "The reference sequences used for the LDLR and PCSK9 genes were NM_000527.4 and NM_174936.3, respectively. Genomic DNA was extracted from the patients' whole blood using an automated DNA extraction machine (QIAsymphony; QIAGEN, Valencia, CA). All coding regions and the exon-intron boundary sequence of the LDLR and PCSK9 genes were examined as described previously. Some patients gave the appearance of homozygosity in exon 1 of the PCSK9 gene due to the presence of single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) of the primer site. All primers in the LDLR and PCSK9 genes were checked for SNPs. In exons 4 and 11 of the PCSK9 gene, the presence of SNPs of primer site was confirmed. In the LDLR gene, there were no SNPs in the primer sites, but the PCR efficiency was suboptimal for exons 3 and 17. Thus, we designed and used new primers for exons 3 and 17 of the LDLR gene and exons 1, 4, and 11 of the PCSK9 gene from February 2017. Patients who underwent genetic analysis before February 2017 and had the appearance of homozygosity in exon 1 or were clinically diagnosed as FH but did not have any pathogenic variants in the LDLR and PCSK9 genes underwent genetic analysis again using the re-designed primers for the PCSK9 gene. Multiplex ligationdependent probe amplification was performed to detect large rearrangements of the LDLR gene using a P062B LDLR MLPA kit (MRC Holland, Amsterdam, the Netherlands). We did not examine any APOB variants in the present study, because variants in the APOB gene have not been reported in Japanese FH patients.", "description": "The input text outlines the DNA analysis methodology (Section 2.2) employed for 801 clinically diagnosed HeFH patients in the study. Reference sequences NM_000527.4 and NM_174936.3 were utilized for the LDLR and PCSK9 genes, respectively. Genomic DNA was extracted from whole blood using the QIAsymphony automated machine. All coding regions and exon-intron boundary sequences of LDLR and PCSK9 were examined. Technical challenges included SNPs in PCSK9 primer sites (exons 1, 4, 11) causing apparent homozygosity and suboptimal PCR efficiency for LDLR exons 3 and 17. To address this, new primers for LDLR exons 3 and 17 and PCSK9 exons 1, 4, and 11 were designed and used from February 2017. Patients analyzed before February 2017 with apparent homozygosity in PCSK9 exon 1 or no pathogenic variants despite clinical FH diagnosis underwent re-analysis with redesigned PCSK9 primers. Large LDLR rearrangements were detected using MLPA with the P062B LDLR MLPA kit. APOB variants were not examined as they have not been reported in Japanese FH patients, consistent with the study's Introduction."}

e = {"title": "Impact of LDLR and PCSK9 pathogenic variants in Japanese heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia patients", "authors": ["Mika Hori", "Naotaka Ohta", "Atsushi Takahashi", "Hiroaki Masuda", "Rieko Isoda", "Suguru Yamamoto", "Cheol Son", "Masatsune Ogura", "Kiminori Hosoda", "Yoshihiro Miyamoto", "Mariko Harada-Shiba"], "section_header": "2. Materials and methods", "subsection_header": "2.3. Classification of pathogenic variants", "sub_subsection_header": "null", "genes_mentioned": ["LDLR", "PCSK9"], "variant_count": 0, "variants": [], "path": "/Users/arjun/PycharmProjects/VI_RAG/VI_RAG/tools/Hori_et_al_2019_PMID_31491741.md", "chunk_idx": 1, "chunk": "In the present study, only nonsynonymous variants were defined as variants. The variants detected in the LDLR or PCSK9 gene were classified as pathogenic or likely pathogenic using ClinVar; the LOVD database; population data from the Exome Aggregation Consortium (ExAC), the Japanese Human Genetic Variation Database, and the Tohoku Medical Megabank Organization; in silico tools/software; and functional data based on guidelines edited by the American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics (ACMG) and the Association for Molecular Pathology (AMP). If a variant was confirmed to co-segregate in at least two or three families with at least two affected family members, the variant was scored as having moderate to strong evidence for pathogenicity.", "description": "The input text delineates the variant classification methodology employed in the referenced study on Japanese heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia (HeFH) patients. It establishes that only nonsynonymous variants were defined as variants, with classification into pathogenic or likely pathogenic categories relying on ClinVar, LOVD, ExAC, Japanese Human Genetic Variation Database, and Tohoku Medical Megabank Organization population data, supplemented by in silico tools and functional data per ACMG/AMP guidelines. Variants demonstrating co-segregation in at least two or three families with at least two affected members were scored as having moderate to strong evidence for pathogenicity. Within the study context, this methodology identified 92 pathogenic or likely pathogenic variants among 137 LDLR variants and 4 pathogenic or likely pathogenic variants among 21 PCSK9 variants. Notable PCSK9 pathogenic variants classified under these rules included c.94G>A:p.(Glu32Lys), c.385G>A:p.(Asp129Asn), c.644G>A:p.(Arg215His), and c.1486C>T:p.(Arg496Trp), while c.10G>A:p.(Val4Ile) was classified as benign. This classification system underpinned the study's conclusion that LDLR and PCSK9 pathogenic variants were found in 46% and 7.8% of unrelated FH patients respectively, enabling annotated clinical significance for genetic diagnosis."}

f = {"title": "Impact of LDLR and PCSK9 pathogenic variants in Japanese heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia patients", "authors": ["Mika Hori", "Naotaka Ohta", "Atsushi Takahashi", "Hiroaki Masuda", "Rieko Isoda", "Suguru Yamamoto", "Cheol Son", "Masatsune Ogura", "Kiminori Hosoda", "Yoshihiro Miyamoto", "Mariko Harada-Shiba"], "section_header": "2. Materials and methods", "subsection_header": "2.4. Clinical and laboratory data", "sub_subsection_header": "null", "genes_mentioned": [], "variant_count": 0, "variants": [], "path": "/Users/arjun/PycharmProjects/VI_RAG/VI_RAG/tools/Hori_et_al_2019_PMID_31491741.md", "chunk_idx": 1, "chunk": "Clinical data were retrospectively collected based on the patient's medical records maintained at the NCVC or another hospital. The serum untreated levels of total cholesterol (TC), triglycerides (TG), and highdensity lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C) were measured using standard methods. Unless the untreated TG levels were greater than 400 mg/dL, the LDL-C levels were calculated using the Friedewald formula. The Achilles tendon thickness (ATT) was measured by X-ray analysis. CAD was evaluated based on the presence of myocardial infarction, angina pectoris, or coronary arteries with>75% stenosis by coronary angiography or electrocardiogram.", "description": "This text outlines the clinical and laboratory data collection protocols detailed in Section 2.4 of the study involving 801 clinically diagnosed Japanese heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia (HeFH) patients. Data were retrospectively gathered from medical records at the National Cerebral and Cardiovascular Center (NCVC) or referring hospitals. Untreated serum levels of total cholesterol (TC), triglycerides (TG), and high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C) were measured using standard methods, while low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C) was calculated via the Friedewald formula unless untreated TG levels exceeded 400 mg/dL. Phenotypic assessments included Achilles tendon thickness (ATT) measured by X-ray analysis and coronary artery disease (CAD) defined by myocardial infarction, angina pectoris, or coronary arteries with >75% stenosis via coronary angiography or electrocardiogram. These methodological standards facilitated the study's analysis associating LDLR and PCSK9 pathogenic variants with clinical characteristics, revealing that patients with LDLR pathogenic variants had significantly higher LDL-C levels and ATT prevalence compared to those with PCSK9 pathogenic variants or no rare variants, and enabled the stratification of CAD onset age relative to genetic status."}

g = {"title": "Impact of LDLR and PCSK9 pathogenic variants in Japanese heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia patients", "authors": ["Mika Hori", "Naotaka Ohta", "Atsushi Takahashi", "Hiroaki Masuda", "Rieko Isoda", "Suguru Yamamoto", "Cheol Son", "Masatsune Ogura", "Kiminori Hosoda", "Yoshihiro Miyamoto", "Mariko Harada-Shiba"], "section_header": "2. Materials and methods", "subsection_header": "2.5. Statistical analyses", "sub_subsection_header": "null", "genes_mentioned": [], "variant_count": 0, "variants": [], "path": "/Users/arjun/PycharmProjects/VI_RAG/VI_RAG/tools/Hori_et_al_2019_PMID_31491741.md", "chunk_idx": 1, "chunk": "Continuous variables are presented as the mean+SD or median (interquartile range), and categorical data are presented as percentages. The statistical significance of the differences between two groups was determined using unpaired Student's t-test or Mann-Whitney U test for continuous variables and Fisher's exact test, logistic regression test, or Cochran-Armitage test for categorical variables. The statistical significance of the differences among multiple groups was determined by one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) or Kruskal-Wallis test followed by the Tukey-Kramer or Steel-Dwass test for continuous variables and by the Pearson Chi-square test followed by Fisher's exact test for categorical variables. The differences in the allele frequency were assessed by Fisher's exact test. p-values<0.05 were considered statistically significant. Bonferroni correction was applied for multiple comparisons of categorical variables. The Cochran-Armitage trend test was performed using R software, and the other statistical analyses were performed using JMP 13.0.0 software (SAS International, Cary, NC, USA).", "description": "This text delineates the statistical analysis methodology (Section 2.5) employed in the study Impact of LDLR and PCSK9 pathogenic variants in Japanese heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia patients to assess associations between genetic variants and clinical phenotypes. Continuous variables were presented as the mean+SD or median (interquartile range), and categorical data were presented as percentages. Statistical significance of differences between two groups was determined using unpaired Student's t-test or Mann-Whitney U test for continuous variables and Fisher's exact test, logistic regression test, or Cochran-Armitage test for categorical variables. Statistical significance of differences among multiple groups was determined by one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) or Kruskal-Wallis test followed by the Tukey-Kramer or Steel-Dwass test for continuous variables and by the Pearson Chi-square test followed by Fisher's exact test for categorical variables. Differences in allele frequency were assessed by Fisher's exact test. p-values<0.05 were considered statistically significant, and Bonferroni correction was applied for multiple comparisons of categorical variables. The Cochran-Armitage trend test was performed using R software, and other statistical analyses were performed using JMP 13.0.0 software. These methods were applied to analyze clinical characteristics including serum lipid levels, Achilles tendon thickness, and coronary artery disease prevalence in 724 HeFH patients aged over 20 years, comparing carriers of LDLR pathogenic variants, PCSK9 pathogenic variants, variants of uncertain significance, and non-carriers of LDLR/PCSK9 rare variants."}

